.TH CARTESIAN 1
.SH NAME
cartesian \- graph a user-entered algebraic equation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B temple/cartesian
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Cartesian
is the TempleOS Cartesian-plot demo: it prompts
.B y=
for an algebraic equation in
.IR x ,
graphs it, and repeats.
It runs under
.IR wm (1).
.PP
Type the right-hand side of the equation (for example
.B 2*x
or
.BR sin(x/20)*100 )
and press Enter.
The expression is compiled with
.IR hoc (2),
so its grammar is hoc's: the operators
.BR + ,
.BR \- ,
.BR * ,
.BR / ,
.BR % ,
.BR ^ ,
the functions
.BR sin ,
.BR cos ,
.BR tan ,
.BR atan ,
.BR asin ,
.BR acos ,
.BR log ,
.BR log10 ,
.BR exp ,
.BR sqrt ,
.BR int ,
.BR abs ,
and the constants
.B PI
and
.BR E .
The variables
.BR x ,
.BR y ,
and
.B y_last
are readable from the expression, as in the original.
.PP
As in TempleOS, the plot is in 1:1 pixel space: as
.I x
runs across the window in pixels, the expression's value is the height in
pixels above the centre, with no automatic scaling \(em
.B 2*x
is a line of slope 2.
Red axes cross the full window through the centre; the curve is drawn
black on the white page, joining successive samples with line segments.
.PP
After the graph is drawn, any key clears the page and prompts for a new
equation.
The controls are:
.TF Enter
.TP
.B Enter
Graph the typed equation; with an empty line, exit
.TP
.B Backspace
Edit the equation
.TP
.B Esc
Quit
.PD
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/temple/cartesian.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR temple (1),
.IR hoc (2),
.IR wm (1)
.SH BUGS
The original JIT-compiles the entered text as HolyC via
.BR ExePrint ;
here the grammar is
.IR hoc (2)'s
expression language, which covers algebraic equations but not arbitrary
HolyC.
